-- Issue 1139, weaker consequence (2) — an EFFECT in a non-final constructor
-- argument.  Same hole as reject_1139_nonfinal_ctor_ref.mdk with the mutable
-- cell swapped for an IO effect: `println "EFFECT"` is an application, hence
-- expansive, and it sits in NON-final position of `Box _ _`.  Before the fix
-- only the final argument (`[]`) was tested, so `b` generalized to `Box a` and
-- could be consumed at `Box Int` AND `Box String` in the same program.
--
-- WHY TWO TYPES: with a one-type use the binding needs no generalization and
-- the fixture would sit green whether or not the hole is open.  The two
-- conflicting instantiations are the entire point — they are what makes the
-- wrongly-generalized scheme observable at the type level.
--
-- The effect is written INLINE inside `main` on purpose.  Hoisting it into a
-- helper (`shout : Unit -> Int` that prints) does NOT work: the effect checker
-- rejects the helper first (`declared with <> but also performs <IO>`) and the
-- probe never reaches the value restriction at all.
--
-- Verdict: REJECT, by a type-mismatch diagnostic at the second instantiation.
data Box a = Box Unit (List a)

useI : Box Int -> Int
useI (Box _ xs) = length xs

useS : Box String -> Int
useS (Box _ xs) = length xs

main =
  let b = Box (println "EFFECT") []
  println (debug (useI b + useS b))
